1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,728
This paying agent is about to send 500 test dollars on Tempo's testnet.

2
00:00:06,778 --> 00:00:12,984
First it asks a preflight provider: will the receiver actually get it?

3
00:00:13,034 --> 00:00:19,762
This provider — I configured it to lie, and it says so — answers "yes,

4
00:00:19,812 --> 00:00:26,540
plus 500", and bonds 500 of its own money behind that answer, on-chain.

5
00:00:26,590 --> 00:00:28,104
The agent pays.

6
00:00:28,154 --> 00:00:32,275
The transaction succeeds — but the receiver gets nothing:

7
00:00:32,325 --> 00:00:39,574
its policy blocks this sender, so Tempo holds the 500 in its receive-policy guard.

8
00:00:39,624 --> 00:00:41,659
The answer was wrong.

9
00:00:41,709 --> 00:00:50,523
A challenger — here, the agent itself — re-runs that transfer with Tempo's own engine inside a zero-knowledge proof:

10
00:00:50,573 --> 00:00:54,172
about eight minutes, locally — sped up here.

11
00:00:54,222 --> 00:01:00,950
The contract verifies the proof, and the provider's bond pays the agent 500.

12
00:01:01,000 --> 00:01:04,194
The same machinery protects an honest server.

13
00:01:04,244 --> 00:01:06,511
Here the answer was right.

14
00:01:06,561 --> 00:01:11,609
A challenge with a real proof — simulated, so nothing is sent

15
00:01:11,659 --> 00:01:17,170
— is rejected: the contract verifies the proof, then sees the answer matches.

16
00:01:17,220 --> 00:01:19,950
A correct answer is never slashed.

17
00:01:20,000 --> 00:01:33,682
Before relying on an answer, the agent's SDK checks five things: the question is the one it asked; the reservation is on-chain, for it; the digest it computes itself matches;

18
00:01:33,732 --> 00:01:49,704
the contract and the proof key are the ones it expects; and it saved the chain's own state proofs — in about nine seconds here — so the server can't make an answer unchallengeable by withholding evidence.

19
00:01:49,754 --> 00:01:52,450
If that fails, it says so.

20
00:01:52,500 --> 00:01:54,288
Why trust the proof?

21
00:01:54,338 --> 00:01:59,342
It runs Tempo's own code — tempo-revm, the engine in Tempo's node,

22
00:01:59,392 --> 00:02:03,477
built for a zero-knowledge VM with three small patches.

23
00:02:03,527 --> 00:02:10,828
I replayed forty real Moderato transfers through it, on state proven against the block before each:

24
00:02:10,878 --> 00:02:16,342
forty of forty match their receipts — status, gas, fees, logs and balances.

25
00:02:16,392 --> 00:02:21,396
Tempo's own Zones verifier says it isn't a proving guest yet.

26
00:02:21,446 --> 00:02:26,450
The contract has no owner, no admin and no upgrade path.

27
00:02:26,500 --> 00:02:32,408
A reservation costs about three hundredths of a cent in fees on Tempo;

28
00:02:32,458 --> 00:02:38,367
the real cost is the server's bond, locked for a day per answer.

29
00:02:38,417 --> 00:02:43,408
It insures the answer about one block — not a later payment.

30
00:02:43,458 --> 00:02:47,992
Next: MPP sellers who want to stand behind their answers.

31
00:02:48,042 --> 00:02:50,283
Open source, on Moderato today.

32
00:02:50,333 --> 00:02:53,950
Sworn — paid answers that can be proven false.
